<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Auto Backup rules for Android 11 and below (API 24-30), via `android:fullBackupContent`.
  The API 31+ counterpart is data_extraction_rules.xml. BOTH are needed: minSdkVersion is 24, and
  each attribute is ignored by the API levels the other one covers.

  ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The per-app cloud backup limit is 25 MB. Past it, Android calls onQuotaExceeded() and backs up
  NOTHING — silently. NavBharatAI ships ~39 MB of web assets and registers a service worker that
  caches the app shell, so the WebView's own caches alone can carry the data directory past that
  limit. The likely state before this file: cloud backup failing entirely, and users quietly having
  to sign in again on a new phone with no explanation.

  So this is not a security tightening. It is what makes backup WORK — and the thing users actually
  want (arrive on a new phone still signed in) depends on it working. It is also a prerequisite for
  the April-2027 Zero-Tap requirement, whose restore key travels by this same mechanism.

  ── WHAT IS EXCLUDED, AND WHAT MUST NEVER BE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  EXCLUDED: WebView cache directories ONLY. A cache is by definition regenerable — dropping it costs
  one re-fetch and nothing else. Restoring a stale service-worker cache onto a new device is in fact
  actively harmful; it is a known source of "the app came back on an old version".

  ⚠️ NOT EXCLUDED, and this is the line to be careful with: `app_webview/Local Storage` and
  `app_webview/.../IndexedDB` hold the Firebase Auth session. Excluding `app_webview/` wholesale —
  the obvious-looking one-liner — would sign out every user who restores a backup. The excludes below
  are therefore narrow and named, never a parent directory.

  Chromium has used two profile layouts over the years (`app_webview/<dir>` and
  `app_webview/Default/<dir>`), so both spellings are listed. An exclude path that matches nothing is
  harmless; a missing one is not.

  Android already excludes getCacheDir(), getCodeCacheDir() and getNoBackupFilesDir() by default —
  none of which is where WebView keeps its caches, which is exactly why these entries are needed.
-->
<full-backup-content>
  <!-- Chromium profile at the app_webview root (older WebView layout). -->
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Cache" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Code Cache" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/GPUCache" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Service Worker/CacheStorage" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Service Worker/ScriptCache" />

  <!-- Chromium profile under Default/ (current WebView layout). -->
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Default/Cache" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Default/Code Cache" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Default/GPUCache" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Default/Service Worker/CacheStorage" />
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Default/Service Worker/ScriptCache" />

  <!-- Capacitor's own HTTP cache, when the WebView writes one outside the profile. -->
  <exclude domain="root" path="app_webview/Default/blob_storage" />
</full-backup-content>
